+#+TITLE: lab-mail: a mail relay jail for p7x.dev
+
+artifact: a jail instance in this dir, built and operated like the
+other instances in =jails/instances=.
+
+goal: agents in jails on beastie send and read p7x.dev mail without
+holding the mail credentials. lab-mail holds them, and it is the only
+thing that talks to the mail provider.
+
+provider: p7x.dev and lists.p7x.dev are hosted on Fastmail.
+- IMAP: imap.fastmail.com, port 993, implicit TLS
+- SMTP submission: smtp.fastmail.com, port 465, implicit TLS
+- Fastmail requires an app password for IMAP and SMTP.
+
+access:
+- lab-mail uses =ip4 = inherit=, like the client jails. A jail with
+  its own address has its own 127.0.0.1, which the other jails cannot
+  reach.
+- lab-mail listens on 127.0.0.1 only: SMTP on port 25, IMAP on port
+  143. It never listens on an external address.
+- So beastie, and every jail on it that uses =ip4 = inherit=, can use
+  lab-mail, and nothing off beastie can. That is the security
+  boundary. No pf rules are needed.
+- Clients do not authenticate to send. IMAP has no anonymous mode,
+  so lab-mail accepts any user name and password at login.
+- No TLS between clients and lab-mail. The traffic stays on loopback.
+
+credentials:
+- The operator creates them on the host in
+  =/jails/data/lab-mail/secrets/=. They never enter the repo.
+- nullfs-mounted read-only into lab-mail, and into no other jail.
+- Inside lab-mail, readable only by the daemons that use them.
+- The instance documents how the operator creates them.
+
+sending:
+- Software: OpenSMTPD (mail/opensmtpd).
+- lab-mail relays the mail it accepts to Fastmail SMTP, logging in
+  with the stored credentials.
+- It checks each envelope recipient (RCPT TO) against this list:
+  - =pat@patmaddox.com=
+  - any address at =p7x.dev=
+  - any address at =lists.p7x.dev=
+- Domains match exactly and without regard to case. =p7x.dev= does
+  not match its subdomains. That is why =lists.p7x.dev= is listed on
+  its own.
+- A recipient that is not on the list is rejected with a 5xx reply
+  to its RCPT TO, so the client sees the rejection in the session.
+  The other recipients of the same message are still accepted.
+- lab-mail does not check or rewrite the sender, the headers, or the
+  body. Fastmail decides which senders it accepts.
+- lab-mail queues mail before relaying it. When Fastmail rejects a
+  message, the client has already received 250. lab-mail then sends a
+  bounce to the envelope sender, which is on the recipient list when
+  the sender is at p7x.dev.
+
+reading:
+- Software: isync (mail/isync) for mbsync, and Dovecot (mail/dovecot)
+  for IMAP.
+- mbsync in lab-mail pulls the Fastmail folder =lists/= into a local
+  maildir at least once a minute. =lists/= is a single folder with no
+  subfolders.
+- mbsync only pulls. It never sends new messages, flag changes, or
+  deletions to Fastmail. This is the read-only guarantee: whatever a
+  client does, nothing reaches Fastmail.
+- The local maildir lives in =/jails/data/lab-mail/mail/=, mounted
+  read-write into lab-mail only, so a rebuild of the jail does not
+  fetch the folder again.
+- Dovecot serves the local maildir as one read-only mailbox named
+  =lists=. Clients can list, fetch, and search it. Attempts to set
+  flags, append, copy into it, or expunge leave the maildir unchanged.
+- Reading keeps working from the local copy while Fastmail is down.
+
+acceptance test: run from lab-claude.
+1. pat@patmaddox.com sends a message to lab@lists.p7x.dev.
+2. Within two minutes the message can be fetched from the =lists=
+   mailbox at 127.0.0.1:143.
+3. The agent replies as claude@p7x.dev to pat@patmaddox.com and
+   lab@lists.p7x.dev, through 127.0.0.1:25.
+4. pat@patmaddox.com receives the reply.
+5. A message to someone@example.com through 127.0.0.1:25 is rejected
+   at RCPT TO.
+6. An IMAP STORE of \Deleted followed by EXPUNGE on =lists= leaves the
+   message in place, both in lab-mail and on Fastmail.
+
+out of scope:
+- The lab-devel list and the process-email workflow. They keep using
+  the maildir in jj.
+- Restricting which jails or local users may use lab-mail.
+- Per-jail sender addresses. For now every client sends as
+  claude@p7x.dev.
+
+questions:
+- Is claude@p7x.dev a sending identity on the Fastmail account?
+  Fastmail is likely to reject a sender it does not know, and with a
+  queuing relay that rejection only comes back as a bounce.
+- Where does mail to claude@p7x.dev land? Bounces go there. If a
+  Fastmail rule files it into =lists/=, the agent sees its bounces.
+- Can a Fastmail app password be limited to SMTP, or to IMAP? If so,
+  lab-mail should hold one of each, so that a leaked credential does
+  as little as possible.
+- How does a client jail read the =lists= mailbox? The agent works
+  from a maildir with notmuch, so lab-claude would need an IMAP client
+  such as mbsync (mail/isync) in its packages.
+- Does anything on beastie already listen on 127.0.0.1:25 or :143?
+  Sendmail did by default before FreeBSD 14.

